WEBVTT

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:07.689
So in the previous
video we were in a target

00:00:07.701 --> 00:00:12.610
script and we were creating
an input line called mouse.

00:00:12.611 --> 00:00:16.720
current so we could just create a nice
debug, so find whether we've clicked

00:00:16.721 --> 00:00:19.617
the mouse or not. No
one to be able to create

00:00:19.629 --> 00:00:22.280
a raycast from the
mouse's position so we

00:00:22.281 --> 00:00:27.120
can visualize in the scene view of a game
view of where the actual clicks are aiming.

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:29.776
So this is a great way
to do lots of different

00:00:29.788 --> 00:00:32.611
detections and you can use
raycasts for a number of things.

00:00:32.612 --> 00:00:37.240
So underneath our debug line for the
mouse that was clicked, so we're going

00:00:37.241 --> 00:00:39.764
to create a ray that
goes from the camera

00:00:39.776 --> 00:00:42.560
through to the mouse's
screen position. So we

00:00:42.561 --> 00:00:48.240
can start this by having a local variable
and that'll be time a ray with a capital,

00:00:48.241 --> 00:00:54.040
we can give a lowercase ray as the variable
name, we'll set the equal to camera dot

00:00:54.041 --> 00:00:56.892
main because as long
as our main camera is

00:00:56.904 --> 00:01:00.040
tagged main, we can
use it, then we'll say dot

00:01:00.089 --> 00:01:03.334
screen point to ray
and what that does is

00:01:03.346 --> 00:01:08.002
converts the pixel position
on the screen into world space.

00:01:08.003 --> 00:01:13.600
So it starts at the camera and goes
through at that pixel. Then in brackets,

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.758
we're going to do a
mouse dot current dot

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:22.000
position dot read value
and open and close the

00:01:22.049 --> 00:01:24.436
parentheses, close
the parentheses at the

00:01:24.448 --> 00:01:27.080
end and add a semicolon.
And what that's then

00:01:27.081 --> 00:01:33.760
doing is returning the current mouse position
in screen pixels as a vector to and the

00:01:33.761 --> 00:01:36.413
read value gets the
current value from the

00:01:36.425 --> 00:01:39.280
input system so we
can actually use it. So we

00:01:39.281 --> 00:01:43.560
want to debug this by drawing the ray on
screen because we wouldn't normally be able

00:01:43.561 --> 00:01:48.200
to see it otherwise. So we can write debug
just like with the log book. In this case,

00:01:48.201 --> 00:01:52.052
we can say draw ray.
So we're going to open up

00:01:52.064 --> 00:01:55.760
the parentheses and
say ray dot origin comma

00:01:58.279 --> 00:02:01.882
ray dot direction
times by 100 F comma

00:02:01.894 --> 00:02:07.050
color with a capital dot red
comma to F with a semicolon.

00:02:07.051 --> 00:02:11.600
So what we take is the star or the
red origin. Then we're going to do the

00:02:11.601 --> 00:02:14.020
raise direction vector
three dot direction.

00:02:14.032 --> 00:02:16.520
And then we take that
direction and scale it

00:02:16.521 --> 00:02:22.607
by a hundred units and then make
that in red and for two seconds to appear.

00:02:22.608 --> 00:02:25.467
So make sure that you save
that out. So when the game's

00:02:25.479 --> 00:02:27.760
playing to see this, you
might need to take off

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:30.444
the 2D mode by clicking
the button at the top.

00:02:30.456 --> 00:02:36.108
And if we just go to the 3D, you can see
that we can press the left click and you can

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:39.480
see the actual lines
that are created on the

00:02:39.481 --> 00:02:42.941
ray cast to where we
suspect that we're clicking.

00:02:42.953 --> 00:02:46.588
As a recap, I want you
to go to your target script.

00:02:46.589 --> 00:02:52.800
And in the update method, within the input
line or the if statement for the input,

00:02:52.801 --> 00:02:56.920
we're just going to go below the debug log.
And we're going to say that the ray that

00:02:56.921 --> 00:02:59.531
we're going to create
is equal to main camera

00:02:59.543 --> 00:03:02.400
dot screen point to ray.
You can have in brackets

00:03:02.401 --> 00:03:05.236
mouse at dot current dot
position dot read value.

00:03:05.248 --> 00:03:07.400
And then we're
going to debug the ray

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:11.579
by saying debug dot
draw ray in brackets red

00:03:11.591 --> 00:03:15.680
origin comma ray dot
direction times 100 comma

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:23.440
color dot red with comma to f. And just one
thing I want to mention is that make sure

00:03:23.441 --> 00:03:26.235
that your main camera
is tagged main camera

00:03:26.247 --> 00:03:29.120
because camera dot
main in the code requires

00:03:29.121 --> 00:03:31.560
this tag to happen, but
that's a work efficiently.